From 7b29a3f2aba0f9fbd9de577ce0a0a1b07d95830c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hauke Mehrtens <hauke@hauke-m.de>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 02:38:24 +0100
Subject: Memcheck: Improve definedness checks for MIPS32 musl string loops

Musl's word-at-a-time string routines can load undefined bytes after
a defined terminator. The MIPS32 compiler can keep the byte-test
constants in registers set in a preceding block, so the literal-based
heuristic misses the loop. GCC uses addition for the byte test in the
OpenWrt build. Clang can use subtraction for the same test.

Use the existing operand-use analysis for 32-bit addition and
subtraction and enable precise 32-bit equality checks on MIPS32 musl
builds. This lets Memcheck determine when the defined terminator is
enough to fix the branch outcome.

Add a regression test for both instruction forms that also checks
that a genuinely undefined branch is still reported. Verified uname,
logread, and the Clang-compiled musl strlen and memchr reproducers on
big-endian MIPS32 OpenWrt.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Hauke Mehrtens <hauke@hauke-m.de>
